<!-- doc/src/sgml/bark.sgml -->

<sect1 id="bark">
<title>BARK Indexes</title>

   <indexterm>
    <primary>index</primary>
    <secondary>BARK</secondary>
   </indexterm>

<sect2 id="bark-intro">
 <title>Introduction</title>

 <para>
  <acronym>BARK</acronym> is a B-tree index access method, selected with
  <literal>USING bark</literal>, that is being developed as a successor to
  the standard B-tree (<xref linkend="btree"/>).  Its tree is built the
  way B-tree's is: a multi-level tree of right-linked pages, searched and
  split with the same Lehman and Yao algorithm, with sorted keys, suffix
  truncation of the separator keys, and heap TIDs as a tiebreaker among
  equal keys.  It uses B-tree's operator classes and answers the same
  queries: equality and range conditions on the indexed columns,
  <literal>IN</literal> lists, row comparisons, ordered scans in either
  direction, index-only scans, bitmap scans, parallel scans and parallel
  builds, unique indexes, multicolumn indexes and
  <literal>INCLUDE</literal> columns.
 </para>

 <para>
  BARK adds the following to what B-tree does; each is described in
  <xref linkend="bark-features"/>:
 </para>

 <itemizedlist>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Keys of any size.  B-tree refuses an index entry larger than about a
    third of a page; BARK stores such an entry on a chain of overflow
    pages, so a column whose values can be several kilobytes can be
    indexed directly.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Sorted duplicates in two forms: a list of heap TIDs, and a compressed
    bitmap of heap TIDs for keys with many rows close together in the
    table, which makes indexes on columns with few distinct values much
    smaller than B-tree's.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Leaf prefix compression, an option that stores the leading bytes that
    a leaf page's keys share once per page.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Nearest-neighbor ordering, <literal>ORDER BY col &lt;~&gt;
    constant</literal>, on <type>smallint</type>, <type>integer</type>
    and <type>bigint</type> columns.
   </para>
  </listitem>
 </itemizedlist>

 <para>
  Consider BARK instead of B-tree for an index whose keys can exceed
  B-tree's size limit, an index with many rows per key, an index on long
  keys that share leading bytes (URLs, paths, prefixed codes), or an
  integer column queried for the nearest values to a constant.  For other
  indexes, BARK and B-tree behave alike.  B-tree remains the right choice
  for a primary key or a unique constraint, which BARK cannot back today,
  and for anything else listed in <xref linkend="bark-limitations"/>.
 </para>

 <para>
  BARK is a new access method, and parts of it are still being built.
  Its on-disk format can still change between development versions; an
  index written in a format the server no longer reads is refused, with
  a hint to rebuild it with <command>REINDEX</command>.  The system
  catalogs and TOAST tables keep using B-tree.
 </para>

 <para>
  An example:
<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE orders (id int, customer int, placed date, note text);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX orders_id_idx ON orders USING bark (id);
CREATE INDEX orders_customer_idx ON orders USING bark (customer);
</programlisting>
 </para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-behavior">
 <title>Behavior of BARK Operator Classes</title>

 <para>
  BARK has no operator families of its own for ordinary data types: each
  BARK operator class is a member of the B-tree operator family of its
  type.  For every B-tree operator class in the system catalogs there is
  a BARK operator class with the same name, operator family, input type,
  storage type and default status, so an index that can be built with
  B-tree can be built with BARK, with the same operator class names:
<programlisting>
CREATE INDEX ON orders USING bark (note text_pattern_ops);
</programlisting>
  Because the operator family is shared, BARK uses B-tree's operators and
  support functions unchanged, and everything <xref linkend="btree-behavior"/>
  says about the operators of a B-tree operator family applies to BARK: the
  five comparison operators, with strategy numbers 1 through 5 as in
  <xref linkend="xindex-btree-strat-table"/>, and the laws they must obey.
  The planner treats a BARK index on a column as it treats a B-tree index:
  the same quals are index conditions (including <literal>LIKE</literal>
  prefixes on the pattern classes, as described in
  <xref linkend="indexes-types-btree"/>), and the index provides the same
  sort orders.
 </para>

 <para>
  BARK adds one strategy to the B-tree families of the integer types:
  strategy 6, the ordering operator <literal>&lt;~&gt;</literal> for
  nearest-neighbor scans (see <xref linkend="bark-features-knn"/>).  It is
  registered for BARK only (B-tree does not use it), with purpose
  <literal>ORDER BY</literal>, for <type>smallint</type>,
  <type>integer</type> and <type>bigint</type>.
 </para>

 <para>
  Cross-type comparisons work as they do for B-tree.  A condition comparing
  an indexed column with a constant of another type of the same operator
  family, such as an <type>integer</type> column with a <type>bigint</type>
  constant, positions and stops the scan through the family's cross-type
  comparison support function for that pair of types.  If the family has
  no such function, the condition is checked against each index entry
  instead.  The elements of an <literal>IN</literal> list or
  <literal>= ANY(<replaceable>array</replaceable>)</literal> of another type
  are sorted and compared through the family's support functions for that
  type.
 </para>

 <para>
  NULLs are stored and sorted as in B-tree: an index column's
  <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> or <literal>NULLS LAST</literal> option
  (by default, <literal>NULLS LAST</literal> for an ascending column and
  <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> for a descending one) says where they go,
  and within a column all NULLs compare equal to each other for the
  purpose of ordering entries.  In a unique index, a key with a NULL in any
  column never conflicts with another key unless the index is declared
  <literal>NULLS NOT DISTINCT</literal>.
 </para>

 <para>
  <literal>IS NULL</literal> and <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal> conditions
  are accepted as index conditions, but at present BARK only uses them to
  filter entries; they do not position or stop the scan, as they do in
  B-tree.  A query such as <literal>WHERE col IS NULL</literal> therefore
  reads every leaf page of an index on <literal>col</literal> (or every leaf
  page in the range that the query's other index conditions select), and
  the planner's cost estimate charges for reading them.
 </para>

 <para>
  New operator classes for scalar types work as for B-tree, with one
  difference: a BARK operator class for a scalar type must be placed in a
  B-tree operator family, which can be created with <literal>CREATE OPERATOR
  FAMILY ... USING btree</literal> and named in the <literal>FAMILY</literal>
  clause of <link linkend="sql-createopclass"><command>CREATE OPERATOR
  CLASS ... USING bark</command></link>.  The class may contain only
  operators with strategy numbers 1 through 5, support functions 1 through
  6, and no <literal>STORAGE</literal> clause, as for B-tree:
<programlisting>
CREATE OPERATOR FAMILY my_int4_ops USING btree;
CREATE OPERATOR CLASS my_int4_ops FOR TYPE int4 USING bark FAMILY my_int4_ops AS
    OPERATOR 1 &lt;, OPERATOR 2 &lt;=, OPERATOR 3 =, OPERATOR 4 &gt;=, OPERATOR 5 &gt;,
    FUNCTION 1 btint4cmp(int4, int4);
</programlisting>
  An operator family that belongs to BARK itself (as
  <command>CREATE OPERATOR CLASS ... USING bark</command> creates when it
  names no existing family) is for multikey operator classes, described in
  <xref linkend="bark-multikey-funcs"/>.  A class there without support
  function 7 is reported invalid by <function>amvalidate</function>, and
  <command>CREATE INDEX</command> refuses to use it: BARK reads the strategy
  numbers of a B-tree family's operators as B-tree's, but a family of its
  own allows any number, so a scalar class there could be misread.  Put a
  scalar operator class in a B-tree operator family, as above.
 </para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-support-funcs">
 <title>BARK Support Functions</title>

 <para>
  A BARK operator class for a scalar type uses B-tree's support functions
  1 through 6, with the meanings and signatures given in
  <xref linkend="btree-support-funcs"/> and
  <xref linkend="xindex-btree-support-table"/>.  Since the operator class
  is in a B-tree operator family, the functions it uses are the family's,
  shared with the B-tree operator class of the same type:
 </para>

 <variablelist>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>order</function> (1)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Required.  BARK orders its entries with it, positions and stops scans
     with it, and uses the family's cross-type comparison functions for
     comparisons with constants of other types.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>sortsupport</function> (2)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Optional.  Used, as for B-tree, by the sort that
     <command>CREATE INDEX</command> performs.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>in_range</function> (3)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Optional.  BARK itself does not call it; it is used by window
     functions, as for B-tree.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>equalimage</function> (4)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Optional.  BARK stores the rows of equal keys in one entry (see
     <xref linkend="bark-features-duplicates"/>) only when every key
     column's operator class has an <function>equalimage</function>
     function that returns true for the column's collation, the rule
     B-tree applies to deduplication.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>options</function> (5)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Optional, as for B-tree.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><function>skipsupport</function> (6)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Optional.  A skip scan over the first index column uses it, when the
     column's operator class has one, to go directly to the next possible
     value of that column; without one, the scan skips past the current
     value instead.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
 </variablelist>

 <sect3 id="bark-multikey-funcs">
  <title>Multikey Operator Classes</title>

  <para>
   A <firstterm>multikey</firstterm> operator class extracts several keys
   from one column value, as a GIN operator class does, and stores one
   index entry for each distinct key of each row; the keys are ordered,
   so the index stays a B-tree over them.  The support functions described
   here are the interface for extension authors.  An index with a multikey
   column is built, maintained and scanned like any other BARK index;
   <xref linkend="bark-multikey-scans"/> describes how a scan reads it.
   The planner does not use such an index to provide a sort order on the
   multikey column or on any column after it.
  </para>

  <para>
   A multikey operator class must be in an operator family of BARK's own,
   not a B-tree family, and it must have support function 7.  Its operators
   may have any strategy number; BARK passes the number to support
   functions 8 and 9 and gives it no other meaning.  Its
   <literal>STORAGE</literal> type is the type of the keys, which may
   differ from the input type.  All of its support functions are
   registered under the class's input type (as GIN's are), including
   support function 1, which compares two keys of the storage type; since
   <command>CREATE OPERATOR CLASS</command> would otherwise register a
   comparator under its own argument types, it must be written as
   <literal>FUNCTION 1 (<replaceable>input type</replaceable>,
   <replaceable>input type</replaceable>)
   <replaceable>comparator</replaceable>(<replaceable>storage type</replaceable>,
   <replaceable>storage type</replaceable>)</literal>.  Support functions 1,
   2, 4, 5 and 6 have B-tree's meanings, over the storage type;
   <function>amvalidate</function> reports a class with
   <function>in_range</function> (3) invalid.
  </para>

  <para>
   The additional support functions are:
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><function>extractValue</function> (7)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Required.  Returns the keys of a column value:
<programlisting>
internal extractValue(<replaceable>value</replaceable>, internal nkeys, internal nullFlags [, internal markers, internal nmarkers])
</programlisting>
      The function takes two to five arguments.  It returns a palloc'd
      array of <type>Datum</type> keys and sets <literal>*nkeys</literal>
      (an <type>int32</type>) to their number; it may set
      <literal>*nullFlags</literal> to a palloc'd <type>bool</type> array
      flagging NULL keys.  The keys may be returned in any order and may
      repeat: BARK sorts them with support function 1 and removes
      duplicates.  A NULL value (for which the function is not called), a
      value with no keys, and a key flagged NULL all become one NULL key,
      so every row has at least one entry.  The last two arguments return
      <firstterm>marker</firstterm> keys: facts about the index rather than
      about the row, which BARK stores once per index, as entries that
      name no row, and never removes.  The function is called with all
      five arguments, the output arguments zeroed first, so a function
      declared with fewer simply does not set the rest.  It must be
      immutable.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><function>extractQuery</function> (8)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Turns an operator's argument into ranges of keys:
<programlisting>
void extractQuery(<replaceable>query</replaceable>, int2 strategy, internal boundaries, internal nboundaries, internal extra, internal recheck [, internal flags])
</programlisting>
      It returns, in <literal>*boundaries</literal> and
      <literal>*nboundaries</literal>, an array of
      <structname>BarkBoundary</structname>, each a lower and an upper
      <structname>BarkSearchElement</structname> (a key of the storage
      type and a B-tree strategy number, 1 through 5; a NULL pointer means
      unbounded on that side, and a single key has
      <literal>=</literal> on both sides); <literal>*extra</literal>, data
      for support function 9; and <literal>*recheck</literal>, whether
      support function 9 must be called on the entries found.  The
      optional seventh argument is a
      <structname>BarkQueryFlags</structname> that BARK zeroes before the
      call: the function sets <structfield>backward</structfield> for an
      ordering operator whose ranges are to be read in descending key
      order, and <structfield>searchnulls</structfield> when the query
      must also read the rows whose only entry is the NULL key.  These
      types are declared in <filename>src/include/access/bark.h</filename>.
      A function written for six arguments never sees the seventh.
      Two ranges returned by one call must not overlap; BARK raises an
      error if they do.  No ranges means that nothing matches.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><function>indexRecheck</function> (9)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Required when support function 8 is present:
<programlisting>
int2 indexRecheck(<replaceable>key</replaceable>, int2 strategy, internal extra)
</programlisting>
      Tests one entry's key and returns 0 (true), 1 (maybe: the row is
      returned and rechecked against the table row) or 2 (false).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><function>extractQuery</function> (10), <function>consistent</function> (11),
     <function>comparePartial</function> (12), <function>triConsistent</function> (13)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional: GIN's support functions 3, 4, 5 and 6, with GIN's
      signatures (see <xref linkend="gin-extensibility"/>), for operator
      classes ported from GIN.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><function>fetch</function> (14)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional:
<programlisting>
<replaceable>input type</replaceable> fetch(<replaceable>key</replaceable>)
</programlisting>
      Reserved: <function>amvalidate</function> accepts it, but BARK does
      not call it.  An index-only scan never returns the multikey column,
      only the other columns: the entries hold keys, not values, and a key
      does not determine its row's value (a row with several keys has an
      entry per key, and the single NULL key stands for a NULL value, an
      empty value and a NULL element alike).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   Support functions 7 through 9 follow the interface of DocumentDB's
   <literal>pg_extended_btree</literal> access method (its support
   functions 101 through 103), so an operator class written for it can be
   ported by renumbering them.  Scans use support functions 8, 9 and 14;
   support functions 10 through 13 are validated by
   <function>amvalidate</function> but not yet used.
  </para>

  <para>
   An index may have at most one multikey column, in any key position;
   the other key columns use ordinary operator classes, and every entry of
   a row carries the same values for them.  An <literal>INCLUDE</literal>
   column is never multikey.  A unique index or an exclusion constraint
   cannot have a multikey column.
  </para>

  <para>
   The test module <filename>src/test/modules/bark_multikey</filename>
   provides C functions for a multikey operator class over
   <type>integer[]</type> whose keys are the array's elements.  With that
   module's extension installed, such a class is declared and used like
   this:
<programlisting>
CREATE OPERATOR FAMILY my_int4_array_ops USING bark;
CREATE OPERATOR CLASS my_int4_array_ops
FOR TYPE int4[] USING bark FAMILY my_int4_array_ops AS
    OPERATOR 1 &amp;&amp; (anyarray, anyarray),
    OPERATOR 2 @&gt; (anyarray, anyarray),
    OPERATOR 3 &lt;@ (anyarray, anyarray),
    OPERATOR 4 = (anyarray, anyarray),
    FUNCTION 1 (int4[], int4[]) btint4cmp(int4, int4),
    FUNCTION 2 btint4sortsupport(internal),
    FUNCTION 4 btequalimage(oid),
    FUNCTION 6 btint4skipsupport(internal),
    FUNCTION 7 bark_multikey_extract_value(int4[], internal, internal),
    FUNCTION 8 bark_multikey_extract_query(int4[], int2, internal, internal,
                                           internal, internal, internal),
    FUNCTION 9 bark_multikey_recheck(int4, int2, internal),
    STORAGE int4;

CREATE TABLE tagged (id int, tags int4[]);
INSERT INTO tagged VALUES (1, '{1,2,3}'), (2, '{2}'), (3, '{}'), (4, NULL);
CREATE INDEX tagged_tags_idx ON tagged USING bark (tags my_int4_array_ops);
</programlisting>
   The index holds six (key, row) pairs: one for each element of the
   first two rows, and the NULL key for each of the other two.  Rows with
   equal keys share entries (see <xref linkend="bark-features-duplicates"/>),
   so these six are stored in four entries.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-multikey-scans">
  <title>Scanning a Multikey Index</title>

  <para>
   A scan calls support function 8 for the first condition on the
   multikey column and reads the ranges of keys it returns.  When every
   column before the multikey one has an equality condition (or an
   <literal>IN</literal> list), the scan descends to each range in turn and
   reads only the entries inside it, as it does for the elements of an
   <literal>IN</literal> list; otherwise it reads the entries the other
   conditions allow and keeps those inside a range.  For an entry inside a
   range whose function 8 asked for a recheck, support function 9 decides
   whether the row is returned, dropped, or returned to be rechecked
   against the table row.  A condition <literal>col = ANY
   (<replaceable>array</replaceable>)</literal> combines the ranges of each
   array element; ranges of different elements that overlap are merged,
   and their rows are rechecked rather than passed to support function 9.
   Further conditions on the multikey column do not narrow the ranges,
   since a row may satisfy two conditions through two different keys; the
   rows the scan returns are rechecked against all of them.
  </para>

  <para>
   The NULL key stands for a NULL value, a value with no keys and a key
   flagged NULL alike, so a scan reads the NULL entries only for
   <literal>IS NULL</literal>, for <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal> (which
   also reads every key), or when support function 8 set
   <structfield>searchnulls</structfield>, and it returns their rows to be
   rechecked.  With the test module's class, for example,
   <literal>tags &lt;@ '{1,2}'</literal>, <literal>tags @&gt; '{}'</literal>
   and <literal>tags = '{}'</literal> must find the empty array, which has
   only the NULL key.
  </para>

  <para>
   A row with several keys can be inside the ranges several times.  An
   index scan or index-only scan returns it at its first entry and skips
   the rest, keeping the rows it has returned in a set in memory; the set
   is emptied whenever the columns before the multikey one change value,
   since a row's entries all have the same values there.  The set is not
   needed, and not built, when the index has never held a row with more
   than one entry, or when the condition is a single key; a bitmap scan
   never needs it.  The set may grow past <xref linkend="guc-work-mem"/>
   (times <xref linkend="guc-hash-mem-multiplier"/>) rather than return a
   row twice.  A merge join's restore to a mark takes the rows returned
   after the mark out of the set, so they are returned again.
  </para>

  <para>
   Because of the set, a scan of a multikey index cannot change
   direction, and such an index reports
   <literal>pg_index_has_property(index, 'backward_scan')</literal> false.
   A cursor declared <literal>SCROLL</literal> over it reads through a
   <literal>Materialize</literal> node, which can go back; a cursor
   declared without <literal>SCROLL</literal> can then only fetch forward,
   as over a GIN index.  The planner never chooses a parallel index scan
   of a multikey index; a parallel bitmap heap scan works as usual.
   Since the index orders a multikey column's keys rather than its values,
   <function>pg_index_column_has_property</function> reports
   <literal>orderable</literal>, <literal>asc</literal>,
   <literal>desc</literal>, <literal>nulls_first</literal> and
   <literal>nulls_last</literal> false for that column and for every key
   column after it, and <literal>returnable</literal> false for the
   multikey column; <literal>distance_orderable</literal> is true for a
   leading column with an ordering operator such as
   <literal>&lt;~&gt;</literal>.
   An ordering operator of the class, on a leading multikey column, orders
   rows as MongoDB sorts arrays: the scan walks the column's keys in the
   direction the class's support function 8 asks for, ascending or
   descending, and returns each row once, at its first key, so an ascending
   order sees a row at its smallest element and a descending one at its
   largest.  The operator must return the class's storage type, and its
   NULL result (a row with no key) comes last.  A <literal>WHERE</literal>
   condition on the column does not change that order: the scan reads the
   whole column and each row is rechecked against the condition.  An
   ordering operator on an ordinary leading column (<literal>ORDER BY
   tenant &lt;~&gt; 3</literal>) also returns each row once.  A bitmap
   scan has none of these limits.
  </para>
 </sect3>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-features">
 <title>Features Beyond B-Tree</title>

 <sect3 id="bark-features-oversized">
  <title>Keys of Any Size</title>

  <para>
   An index entry that is too large to be stored on a page (larger than
   about a third of a page after TOAST compression, the limit at which
   B-tree raises an error) is stored by BARK as an
   <literal>OVERSIZED</literal> entry: the complete index tuple is written
   to a chain of overflow pages, and the leaf page holds a small entry that
   points to the chain.  Comparisons, and so searches, scans and the
   uniqueness check, read the full value from the chain, and index-only
   scans return it, so an index on such a column behaves as it would if the
   values were small.  <command>VACUUM</command> frees the chain of an
   entry it removes:
<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE docs (id int, body text);
CREATE INDEX docs_body_idx ON docs USING bark (body);
</programlisting>
   The cost is extra page reads: each comparison with an oversized entry
   reads its overflow chain (and decompresses the value, if it is
   compressed), so lookups and builds over such keys are several times
   slower than over small keys, and every row with an oversized key has a
   chain of its own, even when it repeats another row's key.  For a
   <type>text</type> first column under the <literal>C</literal> or
   <literal>POSIX</literal> collation, the leaf entry also keeps the value's
   first 32 bytes, so two oversized entries that differ there are ordered
   without reading their chains.  The planner's cost estimate charges a
   scan for the chains, from the index's average entry size.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-features-duplicates">
  <title>Sorted Duplicates</title>

  <para>
   Like B-tree with deduplication (see <xref linkend="btree-deduplication"/>),
   BARK can store the rows of one key in one leaf entry that holds the key
   once and the heap TIDs of the rows in ascending order.  Such an entry
   has one of two forms, chosen for each entry by size:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     A <literal>LIST</literal> holds the heap TIDs as an array, six bytes
     each.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     A <literal>POSTING</literal> entry holds them as a compressed bitmap.
     When many rows of the key are close together in the table, it takes a
     fraction of a byte per row.  It is sized so that removing any of its
     rows during <command>VACUUM</command> never makes it larger, and for
     scattered rows that size is larger than a list's, so an entry is made a
     <literal>POSTING</literal> entry only when that is the smaller of the
     two.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   A key with one row is a <literal>SINGLE</literal> entry, B-tree's form.
   An entry of either kind holds at most about a third of a page; a key
   with more rows than that has several entries, in heap TID order.
   <command>CREATE INDEX</command> forms entries of up to about a tenth of a
   page, so that later inserts have room to grow them, and an insert adds
   its row to the entry of its key that its heap TID falls in or follows,
   when there is room.  Before a full leaf page is split, BARK first merges
   adjacent entries of each key on it into fewer, larger entries, as
   B-tree runs a deduplication pass, and splits only if that does not make
   room.
  </para>

  <para>
   Rows are combined into one entry only in an index that is not unique,
   has no <literal>INCLUDE</literal> columns, and whose key columns' operator
   classes all report, through their <function>equalimage</function>
   support function, that equal values have identical stored forms: the
   rule B-tree applies to deduplication.  An index-only scan returns the
   stored key for every row of the entry, so an index on
   <type>numeric</type> (where <literal>1.0 = 1.00</literal>), or on text with a
   nondeterministic collation, keeps one entry per row.  The
   <structfield>allequalimage</structfield> column of
   <function>bark_metap</function> (<xref linkend="pageinspect"/>) shows
   the result.  There is no storage parameter that turns this off.
  </para>

  <para>
   For example, in the first table below each value's rows are spread over
   the whole table, and in the second each value's rows are together:
<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE spread (kind int, at int);
INSERT INTO spread SELECT g % 100, g FROM generate_series(1, 1000000) g;
CREATE INDEX spread_kind_idx ON spread USING bark (kind);

CREATE TABLE together (kind int, at int);
INSERT INTO together SELECT g / 10000, g FROM generate_series(1, 1000000) g;
CREATE INDEX together_kind_idx ON together USING bark (kind);
</programlisting>
   The first index is built from <literal>LIST</literal> entries and takes
   766 pages; the second is built from <literal>POSTING</literal> entries
   and takes 28.  A B-tree index on either column takes 852 pages.
   <function>pgstatbarkindex</function> (<xref linkend="pgstattuple"/>)
   counts an index's entries of each form.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-features-prefix">
  <title>Leaf Prefix Compression</title>

  <para>
   With the storage parameter <literal>prefix_compression</literal> on, a
   leaf page of an index whose first column has a variable-length type may
   store a prefix: leading bytes of the first column that its entries
   share, stored once on the page.  Each entry then stores the first
   column as the number of prefix bytes it shares followed by the rest of
   the value.  A page is given a prefix (of at most 126 bytes) only when it
   is written whole, by <command>CREATE INDEX</command> or by a page split,
   and only when the keys share at least 4 leading bytes on average (during
   <command>CREATE INDEX</command>, judged from the keys of the page
   before); the prefix never changes afterward, and an entry inserted later
   that shares less of it is still stored correctly.  Comparisons see the full value,
   so any collation works.  On keys such as URLs or file paths this makes
   the leaf level much smaller; on keys with little in common it changes
   nothing.  Changing the parameter affects only pages written afterward.
   For example, these 200,000 URLs share their first 46 bytes:
<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE urls (url text COLLATE "C");
INSERT INTO urls SELECT 'https://www.example.com/catalog/products/item-' || g
  FROM generate_series(1, 200000) g;
CREATE INDEX urls_url_idx ON urls USING bark (url) WITH (prefix_compression = on);
</programlisting>
   The index takes 559 pages, against 1877 without the parameter and 1895
   for a B-tree index.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-features-knn">
  <title>Nearest-Neighbor Ordering</title>

  <para>
   BARK answers <literal>ORDER BY <replaceable>column</replaceable> &lt;~&gt;
   <replaceable>constant</replaceable></literal> for a
   <type>smallint</type>, <type>integer</type> or <type>bigint</type>
   column that is the index's first column.  The operator
   <literal>&lt;~&gt;</literal> returns the absolute difference of its
   operands as <type>double precision</type>.  The scan descends to the
   constant and reads outward in both directions, returning rows in order
   of increasing distance, so a query with a <literal>LIMIT</literal>
   reads only the pages around the constant:
<programlisting>
SELECT id, id &lt;~&gt; 5000 AS distance FROM orders ORDER BY id &lt;~&gt; 5000 LIMIT 3;
  id  | distance
------+----------
 5000 |        0
 5001 |        1
 4999 |        1
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
   Rows with a NULL key come last.  Only one such ordering operator is used
   per scan.  A nearest-neighbor scan is never parallel, and it can be
   combined with other index conditions, which then filter the rows it
   reads.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-features-bloat">
  <title>Bloat Under Churn</title>

  <para>
   Rows inserted after a <command>VACUUM</command> reuse the space it freed
   in the table, so their heap TIDs fall inside the TID ranges of existing
   entries of their keys.  BARK adds such a row to the entry whose range it
   falls in, or, when that entry is full, divides the entry in two around
   it, and when the page is full it merges the page's entries of each key
   before splitting the page.  So in an index that combines rows into
   <literal>LIST</literal> and <literal>POSTING</literal> entries, a cycle
   of deletes, <command>VACUUM</command> and inserts of keys with many rows
   each leaves the index much less bloated than the same cycle leaves a
   B-tree index; for keys with one row each, the two grow alike.
   Bottom-up deletion (see <xref linkend="bark-implementation"/>) limits
   the growth caused by <command>UPDATE</command>s, as it does for
   B-tree.
  </para>
 </sect3>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-storage-params">
 <title>Storage Parameters</title>

 <para>
  BARK indexes accept these storage parameters, set with
  <literal>WITH</literal> in <link linkend="sql-createindex"><command>CREATE
  INDEX</command></link> or changed with <link
  linkend="sql-alterindex"><command>ALTER INDEX ... SET</command></link>:
 </para>

 <variablelist>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><literal>fillfactor</literal> (<type>integer</type>)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The percentage to which <command>CREATE INDEX</command> fills leaf
     pages, and to which a split of the rightmost leaf page leaves its left
     half, so that ascending inserts fill pages as the build does.  Other
     leaf splits divide a page's contents about evenly, except that a page
     holding a single key that no page to its right holds is left 96% full,
     since no other key can be inserted into it.  Internal pages are filled
     to 70% by <command>CREATE INDEX</command> and by a split of the
     rightmost internal page of a level, and divided evenly otherwise,
     whatever the parameter's value.  The default is 90; any value from 10 to 100 can be
     selected.  It has the meaning it has for B-tree (see
     <xref linkend="index-reloption-fillfactor"/>), and a change affects
     only pages written afterward.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
  <varlistentry>
   <term><literal>prefix_compression</literal> (<type>boolean</type>)</term>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Whether leaf pages store a prefix shared by their keys (see
     <xref linkend="bark-features-prefix"/>).  It applies only when the
     index's first column has a variable-length type.  The default is
     <literal>off</literal>.  A change affects only pages written
     afterward; <command>REINDEX</command> rewrites every page.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </varlistentry>
 </variablelist>

 <para>
  B-tree's <literal>deduplicate_items</literal> parameter does not exist
  for BARK: whether rows of a key share an entry is decided by the rule in
  <xref linkend="bark-features-duplicates"/>.
 </para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-implementation">
 <title>Implementation</title>

 <para>
  This section summarizes how BARK works, for advanced users.  See
  <filename>src/backend/access/bark/README</filename> in the source
  distribution for the full description.
 </para>

 <sect3 id="bark-structure">
  <title>Structure</title>

  <para>
   A BARK index has B-tree's structure (see <xref linkend="btree-structure"/>):
   block 0 is a metapage, which records the format version and the root;
   every other page is a leaf page, an internal page, an overflow page
   holding part of an oversized entry, or a deleted page awaiting reuse.
   The pages of each level are linked to their left and right siblings,
   and each page has a high key, an upper bound on the keys it may hold.
   Searches use the Lehman and Yao algorithm, as B-tree's do: a search that
   finds a page split since it read the page's parent moves right along
   the sibling links, so readers hold only one page lock at a time and
   never block on a split.  A split of a page is completed by inserting a
   downlink to the new page into the parent; if a split is interrupted by
   an error or a crash, the next insert that reaches the page completes
   it.  BARK pages carry a page type identifier distinct from B-tree's, so
   tools can tell them apart.
  </para>

  <para>
   Separator keys are truncated, as in B-tree: a high key or downlink keeps
   only the leading key columns needed to separate the two pages it lies
   between, and never <literal>INCLUDE</literal> columns.  Within the
   entries of one key, leaf entries are ordered by heap TID, and a
   separator between two entries of one key carries a heap TID.  An insert
   therefore descends directly to the one leaf page where its entry
   belongs, as in B-tree since <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> 12.
   An index built by a development version of BARK from before entries
   were ordered by heap TID is refused with a hint to rebuild it with
   <command>REINDEX</command>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Inserts of ascending keys into a large index go directly to the
   rightmost leaf page, without a descent, while the keys keep arriving in
   order, as in B-tree.  In a unique index, an insert first checks for a
   live row with an equal key, waiting for a transaction that is inserting
   or deleting one to finish; <literal>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT</literal>
   waits only for the conflicting insertion itself to be confirmed or
   withdrawn.  <command>CREATE INDEX</command> sorts the table's rows and
   writes the tree from the bottom up, and can use parallel workers for the
   table scan and sort.
  </para>

  <para>
   As in B-tree, <command>CREATE INDEX</command> and
   <command>REINDEX</command> also index row versions that have been
   deleted or updated but that a transaction older than the build may
   still see (<quote>recently dead</quote> rows), so that such a
   transaction gets the same answer from the new index as from the table.
   These row versions are left out of a unique index's uniqueness check: a
   deleted row and a live row with the same key do not make the build of a
   unique index fail, while two live rows with the same key do.  Nothing in
   the index records which entries came from such rows; the table decides
   their visibility, as for every other entry.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-deletion">
  <title>Deletion and VACUUM</title>

  <para>
   BARK performs bottom-up index deletion as B-tree does (see
   <xref linkend="btree-deletion"/>): when an <command>UPDATE</command>
   that did not change the index's columns would split a leaf page, BARK
   first asks the table which of the page's row versions are dead to
   every transaction and removes their entries, and splits only if that
   does not make room.  Unlike B-tree, BARK does not mark entries dead
   when a scan finds their rows dead, and so has no simple deletion of
   such entries before a split: the entries of dead rows are removed only
   by bottom-up deletion and by <command>VACUUM</command>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>VACUUM</command> reads the index once in physical order, as for
   B-tree, removing the entries of dead rows (and the rows' heap TIDs from
   <literal>LIST</literal> and <literal>POSTING</literal> entries, which it
   rewrites in place) and freeing the overflow chains of the oversized
   entries it removes.  Leaf pages split during the pass are revisited
   when their new pages lie behind the pass, using B-tree's vacuum cycle
   IDs.  It deletes an emptied leaf page that has siblings on both sides
   and is not the last child of its parent, passing its key range to its
   right sibling; an empty leaf at either end of the leaf level, or the
   last child of its parent, stays in place, and internal pages are never
   deleted.  A
   deleted page keeps its sibling links, so a scan that was moving to it
   moves on, and it is reused only once no transaction can still hold a
   link to it.  <command>VACUUM</command> can process BARK indexes in
   parallel workers.
  </para>
 </sect3>

 <sect3 id="bark-wal">
  <title>WAL and Hot Standby</title>

  <para>
   BARK has its own WAL resource manager, <literal>bark</literal>, with a
   record type for each change: leaf inserts, additions to
   <literal>LIST</literal> and <literal>POSTING</literal> entries, page
   splits, new roots, overflow pages, merges, bottom-up deletion,
   <command>VACUUM</command>, page deletion and page reuse.  It can be
   named in <xref linkend="guc-wal-consistency-checking"/>.  BARK indexes
   are crash-safe and are replicated to standby servers.  On a hot standby,
   replay of bottom-up deletion and of the reuse of a deleted page cancels
   the queries that could still need what is removed, and replay of
   <command>VACUUM</command>'s changes waits for scans that hold a pin on
   the page, as for B-tree (see <xref linkend="hot-standby-conflict"/>).
  </para>
 </sect3>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-tools">
 <title>Tools</title>

 <para>
  These contributed modules and programs know BARK indexes:
 </para>

 <itemizedlist>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <xref linkend="pageinspect"/> has <function>bark_metap</function>,
    <function>bark_page_stats</function>,
    <function>bark_multi_page_stats</function> and
    <function>bark_page_items</function>, the counterparts of its B-tree
    functions, which show the metapage, a page's summary and a page's
    entries with their forms (<literal>SINGLE</literal>,
    <literal>LIST</literal>, <literal>POSTING</literal>,
    <literal>OVERSIZED</literal>, <literal>PIVOT</literal>,
    <literal>PREFIX</literal>); see <xref linkend="pageinspect-bark-funcs"/>.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <xref linkend="pgstattuple"/> has <function>pgstatbarkindex</function>,
    which reports what <function>pgstatindex</function> reports for a
    B-tree index, plus the number of overflow pages and of leaf entries of
    each form.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <xref linkend="amcheck"/> has
    <function>bark_index_check(index regclass, heapallindexed
    boolean)</function>, which checks the structure of each page and the
    links between pages and, with <literal>heapallindexed</literal>, that
    every row of the table that the index should hold has an entry; and
    <function>bark_index_parent_check(index regclass, heapallindexed
    boolean)</function>, which also checks each level of the tree against
    the level below.  <literal>heapallindexed</literal> is not supported
    for an index with a multikey column.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <xref linkend="app-pgamcheck"/> checks BARK indexes along with B-tree
    indexes, using <function>bark_index_check</function>, or
    <function>bark_index_parent_check</function> with
    <option>--parent-check</option>, when the database's
    <application>amcheck</application> is version 1.7 or later.
   </para>
  </listitem>
 </itemizedlist>

 <para>
  For example:
<programlisting>
SELECT bark_index_check('orders_customer_idx', true);
SELECT single_entries, list_entries, posting_entries, oversized_entries
  FROM pgstatbarkindex('orders_customer_idx');
</programlisting>
 </para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="bark-limitations">
 <title>Limitations and Differences from B-Tree</title>

 <para>
  At present, a BARK index differs from a B-tree index in these ways:
 </para>

 <itemizedlist>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    A <literal>PRIMARY KEY</literal> or <literal>UNIQUE</literal> constraint
    cannot use a BARK index.  Such constraints always create a B-tree
    index, and <literal>ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ... PRIMARY KEY USING
    INDEX</literal> (or <literal>UNIQUE USING INDEX</literal>) refuses a BARK
    index.  A unique BARK index made with <command>CREATE UNIQUE
    INDEX</command> enforces uniqueness, and it can be the target of a
    foreign key, an <literal>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT</literal> arbiter and a
    replica identity.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Since only a constraint can be deferrable, a BARK index cannot
    enforce a deferrable uniqueness constraint.  An exclusion constraint
    (<literal>EXCLUDE USING bark</literal>) works, deferrable or not.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <literal>IS NULL</literal> and <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal> filter
    entries but do not bound the scan (see <xref linkend="bark-behavior"/>).
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    A skip scan, which reads an index on <literal>(a, b)</literal> for a
    condition on <literal>b</literal> alone by skipping from one value of
    <literal>a</literal> to the next, runs forward only, and only over the
    first column.  A backward scan with such a condition reads every entry
    in its range, and B-tree also skips over several leading columns.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    Scans do not mark the entries of dead rows, so later scans keep
    reading them until bottom-up deletion or <command>VACUUM</command>
    removes them (see <xref linkend="bark-deletion"/>).
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <command>CLUSTER</command> and <command>REPACK</command> using a BARK
    index read the table through the index, rather than sorting it as
    they can with a B-tree index.
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    A scan of an index with a multikey column cannot change direction
    (a scrollable cursor is materialized), nor be a parallel index scan,
    nor order by an operator on the multikey column (see
    <xref linkend="bark-multikey-scans"/>).
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    The indexes of the system catalogs and of TOAST tables remain B-tree
    indexes.
   </para>
  </listitem>
 </itemizedlist>

</sect2>

</sect1>
